1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,040
Listen to a talk in an anthropology class.

2
00:00:03,040 --> 00:00:09,200
The potlatch is a ceremonial event among Indigenous peoples of the Pacific Northwest coast of North America.

3
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:14,873
A potlatch involves the host giving away their possessions, including food, blankets,

4
00:00:14,873 --> 00:00:16,180
and even money, to guests.

5
00:00:16,180 --> 00:00:25,000
This practice serves multiple social functions, like redistributing wealth, reinforcing social bonds, and asserting status.

6
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:29,703
Potlatches are often held to mark significant occasions, like weddings and such,

7
00:00:29,703 --> 00:00:33,200
and they involve feasting, dancing, and storytelling.

8
00:00:33,200 --> 00:00:40,030
The lavishness of a potlatch can elevate the host’s status by demonstrating wealth and generosity.

9
00:00:40,030 --> 00:00:44,033
One fascinating aspect of the potlatch is its role in the social

10
00:00:44,033 --> 00:00:47,130
and economic structures of the communities that practice it.

11
00:00:47,130 --> 00:00:55,001
By redistributing wealth, potlatches ensure that resources circulate within the community, preventing the concentration

12
00:00:55,001 --> 00:00:57,130
of wealth in the hands of a few individuals.

13
00:00:57,130 --> 00:01:02,290
This practice strengthens community ties and fosters mutual support.

14
00:01:02,290 --> 00:01:07,172
For people who don't understand the function of potlatches, they may appear to be

15
00:01:07,172 --> 00:01:09,000
a waste of resources.

16
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:13,941
Potlatches were even prohibited by the Canadian government in the late-nineteenth century

17
00:01:13,941 --> 00:01:16,110
because of this perceived wastefulness.

18
00:01:16,110 --> 00:01:19,067
The ban was lifted in the mid-twentieth century,

19
00:01:19,067 --> 00:01:23,110
and today, potlatches continue to be an important cultural practice.

20
00:01:23,110 --> 00:01:23,160
Let's now examine how Indigenous communities have revived this practice.
